  "biography": {
    "plain_text": "Chester Thorne was an American banker who brought inherited family wealth to Tacoma and enlarged it through banking and regional investment. A Yale engineering graduate, he became president of the National Bank of Commerce and later chairman of its successor. He also served among the Port of Tacoma's first commissioners.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 85,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Contemporary inheritance record, bank resolutions and official port history establish the concise account."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S1",
      "S2",
      "S5"
    ],
    "word_count": 50,
    "html": "<p>Chester Thorne was an American banker who brought inherited family wealth to Tacoma and enlarged it through banking and regional investment. A Yale engineering graduate, he became president of the National Bank of Commerce and later chairman of its successor. He also served among the Port of Tacoma's first commissioners.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "During the financial panic of 1893, Tacoma banker Chester Thorne committed his own money to keep the National Bank of Commerce open. Newly appointed president, he faced impaired capital and a city where banks were failing. He raised funds from his private fortune when borrowing was unavailable, protecting the institution through the crisis.\n\nHis interest in English country houses took physical form at Thornewood, beside American Lake. Working with his wife, Anna, and architect Kirtland Kelsey Cutter, he commissioned a Tudor Gothic home using materials brought from Britain. The shipments travelled around Cape Horn to Washington, giving the estate a connection to the buildings that had inspired him.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 85,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Bank's contemporary account establishes the rescue mechanism; estate history documents architectural collaboration and imported materials."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S1",
      "S6"
    ],
    "word_count": 108,
    "html": "<p>During the financial panic of 1893, Tacoma banker Chester Thorne committed his own money to keep the National Bank of Commerce open. Newly appointed president, he faced impaired capital and a city where banks were failing. He raised funds from his private fortune when borrowing was unavailable, protecting the institution through the crisis.</p>\r\n<p>His interest in English country houses took physical form at Thornewood, beside American Lake. Working with his wife, Anna, and architect Kirtland Kelsey Cutter, he commissioned a Tudor Gothic home using materials brought from Britain. The shipments travelled around Cape Horn to Washington, giving the estate a connection to the buildings that had inspired him.</p>\r\n"
  },
